      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62213,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients can present with abdominal pain or distension and androgenic or estrogenic symptoms {29660837; 27751908; 2067122}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62214,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The reported patients with gynandroblastoma have ranged in age from 14 to 80&nbsp;years (median: 24.5&nbsp;years) {29660837}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62215,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62216,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "<p>In the largest study to date, heterozygous hotspot mutations in the RNase&nbsp;IIIb domain of <em>DICER1</em> were discovered in both tumour components in 3 of 16&nbsp;cases, all of which showed an admixture of juvenile granulosa cell tumour and Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumour (SLCT). All tumours were <em>FOXL2</em>-wildtype, including 7&nbsp;cases with an adult granulosa cell tumour component, and none showed a mutation within the pleckstrin-homology domain of <em>AKT1</em> {29660837}. Several additional <em>DICER1</em>-mutant (but only very few <em>FOXL2</em>-mutant) gynandroblastomas have been reported {26428316; 22296244; 29037807}. Therefore, gynandroblastomas containing SLCT and juvenile granulosa cell tumour may represent variant morphologies of pure SLCT, which is characterized by high frequency of mutations in <em>DICER1</em> {22187960}. Gynandroblastoma containing a component of adult granulosa cell tumour seems to be different from pure adult granulosa cell tumour, almost all cases of which harbour hotspot mutations in <em>FOXL2</em> (c.402C&gt;G p.Cys134Trp) {19516027; 27297428}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T19:08:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62217,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are usually unilateral. The average tumour size is 11&nbsp;cm (range: 5.5&ndash;20&nbsp;cm). The cut surface is solid or cystic and pale yellow or white {14479145}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62218,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>These tumours comprise a mixture of female elements (adult or juvenile granulosa cell tumour) and male elements (Sertoli cell tumour or SLCT). The most common combination is a predominant SLCT component and a smaller juvenile granulosa cell tumour component {29660837}. Immunohistochemically, both tumour components are usually positive for sex cord markers, such as inhibin {9688187} and FOXL2 {29660837}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-06T12:38:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62219,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62220,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62221,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a sex cord&ndash;stromal tumour showing an admixture of female and male elements.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62222,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>Benign tumours are not staged. Malignant tumours are staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:21:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62223,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 17,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours are benign. Only rare recurrences have been reported {17197894}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
